Instead of storing prefix counts in a hash table, this version stores prefix XOR values in a binary trie. For a current prefix `p`, a subarray with XOR `k` needs the earlier prefix `p ^ k`. The trie can count that exact value bit by bit.

The trie uses 31 bits, which covers the usual non-negative integer range used by this problem. Each inserted prefix increases the count on its path.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    struct Node {
        int child[2];
        int count;

        Node() {
            child[0] = -1;
            child[1] = -1;
            count = 0;
        }
    };

    vector<Node> trie;

    void insertValue(int value) {
        int node = 0;
        trie[node].count++;

        for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {
            int currentBit = (value >> bit) & 1;
            if (trie[node].child[currentBit] == -1) {
                trie[node].child[currentBit] = (int)trie.size();
                trie.push_back(Node());
            }
            node = trie[node].child[currentBit];
            trie[node].count++;
        }
    }

    int getCount(int value) {
        int node = 0;

        for (int bit = 30; bit >= 0; bit--) {
            int currentBit = (value >> bit) & 1;
            if (trie[node].child[currentBit] == -1) {
                return 0;
            }
            node = trie[node].child[currentBit];
        }

        return trie[node].count;
    }

    int subarraysWithXorK(vector<int> &nums, int k) {
        trie.clear();
        trie.push_back(Node());
        insertValue(0);

        int prefix = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            prefix ^= value;
            answer += getCount(prefix ^ k);
            insertValue(prefix);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

The empty prefix is inserted before reading the array, so a prefix equal to `k` forms a valid subarray from the beginning. The input vector is not changed.

Each trie operation checks 31 bits, so the fixed bit count is treated as a constant here.

Time complexity: `O(31n)`, usually written as `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(31n)`, usually written as `O(n)`.

The prefix XOR idea makes the count much smaller than checking every pair of boundaries. If the current prefix XOR is `prefix`, an earlier prefix must be `prefix ^ k` for their section to have XOR `k`.

The map stores how many times each earlier prefix has appeared. Starting with prefix `0` handles subarrays that begin at index 0.

```java
class Solution {
    public int subarraysWithXorK(int[] nums, int k) {
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> prefixCount = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        prefixCount.put(0, 1);

        int prefix = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            prefix ^= value;

            answer += prefixCount.getOrDefault(prefix ^ k, 0);
            prefixCount.put(prefix, prefixCount.getOrDefault(prefix, 0) + 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

A quick dry run for `[1, 2, 3]`, `k = 2`: the prefix XOR values are `1`, `3`, and `0`. At the second value, `3 ^ 2` is `1`, which was already seen, so the subarray `[2]` is counted. At the last value, `0 ^ 2` is `2`, so no earlier matching prefix is found.

The empty input needs no special branch. The initial map entry is also what makes a matching prefix at the first element count correctly.

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the prefix frequency map.

Kept this version direct. For every starting position, the running XOR is updated while extending the end position. Whenever it becomes `k`, one valid subarray has been found.

This does not need extra memory, so it is useful for checking a faster version. It is slower for large arrays.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int subarraysWithXorK(vector<int> &nums, int k) {
        int answer = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < (int)nums.size(); start++) {
            int currentXor = 0;

            for (int end = start; end < (int)nums.size(); end++) {
                currentXor ^= nums[end];
                if (currentXor == k) {
                    answer++;
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For example, with `nums = [4, 2, 2, 6, 4]` and `k = 6`, the subarrays starting at index 0, 1, and 2 can be counted as the running XOR changes. A single element array also works because the inner loop checks that element directly.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, not counting the input array.

